WEBVTT

00:01.720 --> 00:04.840
[Grace singing]

00:43.440 --> 00:44.720
[song ends]

00:47.960 --> 00:52.160
[men shouting
and woman screaming]

00:52.240 --> 00:53.680
[tires screeching]

00:53.760 --> 00:55.320
[all gasping]

00:57.480 --> 00:58.640
[gunshot]

01:01.320 --> 01:02.560
[Ada screams]

01:02.640 --> 01:06.800
It's all right, Miss Shelby.
We work with Tommy. We're Peaky Blinders.

01:06.880 --> 01:09.000
- [grunts]
- [theme music playing]

01:09.080 --> 01:11.560
My name is not Shelby!

01:27.000 --> 01:28.400
[door opens]

01:28.480 --> 01:30.880
[nurse] Ready for a visitor, Mr. Shelby?

01:31.120 --> 01:32.320
No.

01:32.400 --> 01:34.760
[Campbell]
I'm here on the King's orders,

01:35.600 --> 01:37.400
I'm afraid I must insist.

01:37.920 --> 01:39.200
[door closes]

01:39.280 --> 01:41.080
[Campbell] Ah!

01:41.160 --> 01:43.040
You paid extra for daylight.

01:44.200 --> 01:47.440
The racketeering business
must be booming.

01:47.520 --> 01:48.840
[chuckles]

01:52.120 --> 01:55.160
Are you not going to thank me
for saving your life?

01:59.520 --> 02:00.920
Pass my cigarettes?

02:13.920 --> 02:15.880
Three nights ago,

02:16.400 --> 02:19.080
at the cooperative stables
in Montague Street,

02:19.640 --> 02:21.280
there was a murder.

02:21.360 --> 02:23.600
A man named Duggan.

02:25.360 --> 02:27.680
The Oxfordshire constabulary

02:27.760 --> 02:30.560
found his body
in a shallow grave.

02:32.240 --> 02:33.680
I need to piss.

02:35.040 --> 02:36.200
Nurse?

02:37.200 --> 02:39.160
I know that it was you

02:39.240 --> 02:41.760
who carried out the murder
of Mr. Duggan.

02:46.320 --> 02:47.800
[exhales]

02:49.760 --> 02:51.120
[groans softly]

02:59.160 --> 03:00.360
Oh.

03:01.120 --> 03:02.480
By the way,

03:03.960 --> 03:07.160
Grace, she went to New York.

03:07.240 --> 03:08.800
A place called, uh...

03:10.240 --> 03:11.640
Poughkeepsie.

03:14.040 --> 03:15.560
She's married now.

03:16.520 --> 03:18.800
To a banker. He's rich.

03:18.880 --> 03:20.720
I am sure she is very happy.

03:22.280 --> 03:25.240
You've been under my microscope
for some weeks now.

03:25.320 --> 03:27.840
I have been observing
every move you make.

03:27.920 --> 03:31.360
That's why I was on hand
to save your life.

03:32.880 --> 03:35.720
I imagine being shot by a woman

03:35.840 --> 03:38.840
hurts the same
as being shot by a man.

03:39.560 --> 03:41.240
Just a bit more shameful.

03:42.640 --> 03:44.000
You know, Mr. Campbell,

03:44.440 --> 03:47.560
when I got shot,
they gave me a medal.

03:48.840 --> 03:49.960
Yeah.

03:51.000 --> 03:52.360
No medal for you I bet.

03:54.120 --> 03:55.400
Mr. Shelby.

03:57.000 --> 03:58.360
Our reunion...

04:00.720 --> 04:03.960
is part of a very carefully
worked out plan,

04:04.040 --> 04:07.040
which has been in place
for some time now.

04:07.600 --> 04:09.720
Every time you lean
on that stick,

04:10.360 --> 04:11.840
I bet you see her face.

04:11.920 --> 04:14.920
As a result of the information
in my possession,

04:16.240 --> 04:18.720
I can charge you with murder
at any time

04:18.800 --> 04:22.040
and provide two impeccable crown witnesses

04:22.120 --> 04:26.680
whose testimony will lead you
directly to the gallows.

04:26.760 --> 04:27.880
[gasps]

04:27.960 --> 04:29.560
You are on my hook,

04:30.240 --> 04:32.080
Mr. Shelby.

04:32.160 --> 04:34.280
And from this moment forward,

04:35.800 --> 04:37.560
you belong to me.

04:39.760 --> 04:41.240
[exhales]

04:41.320 --> 04:43.200
So, get well quickly.

04:43.920 --> 04:47.560
I will be in touch the moment
I hear you can piss standing up.

04:47.640 --> 04:50.200
And then I will send you
your instructions.

04:51.960 --> 04:53.000
[door opens]

04:53.080 --> 04:54.440
[exhales]

05:01.960 --> 05:03.200
[loud disturbance]

05:05.120 --> 05:07.560
Curly, you left the gate open?

05:07.640 --> 05:10.480
No. I locked it.
I pinched myself when I did it.

05:10.560 --> 05:12.440
Get this stuff under cover.

05:13.840 --> 05:15.640
- Hey.
- [gate opens]

05:16.200 --> 05:17.560
Who goes there?

05:23.160 --> 05:25.160
- Tommy?
- Curly.

05:25.240 --> 05:27.760
Get the oil you put on
when they go lame.

05:27.840 --> 05:31.080
- Are you fucking mad, Doyle?
- The yellow stuff. Go on.

05:32.360 --> 05:33.760
[groans]

05:33.840 --> 05:36.360
They said you'd be in for another 3 weeks.

05:36.440 --> 05:39.520
I need to get on a boat to London.
Tonight.

05:42.320 --> 05:43.800
You discharged yourself?

05:43.880 --> 05:46.040
I'm a sitting duck in there, Charlie.

05:46.840 --> 05:49.000
Sabini could send a man
at any time.

05:49.080 --> 05:51.040
I need to get some things done.

05:51.120 --> 05:53.240
You're burning up.

05:53.320 --> 05:56.800
Oh, Tommy, that stuff's
for rubbing into fucking horses.

05:56.880 --> 05:57.880
[scoffs]

05:57.960 --> 05:59.640
I am a horse.

05:59.720 --> 06:03.960
If you were a horse, they'd shoot you
with these many broken bones.

06:04.040 --> 06:08.480
Get the black powder, Curly.
Go. The black powder.

06:08.560 --> 06:11.960
You pay for a bloody hospital
and you're using witchcraft?

06:12.360 --> 06:16.360
I need to sleep in the open air
and feel the boat under me, Charlie.

06:16.440 --> 06:18.680
Oh, Tommy...

06:18.760 --> 06:21.840
- You're like your mother.
- Do you have a boat here?

06:23.240 --> 06:25.520
Only the January. She's heavy.

06:25.600 --> 06:27.200
London will take four days.

06:27.280 --> 06:29.160
That'll do.
Can you spare Curly?

06:29.920 --> 06:31.120
He'll be my doctor.

06:31.200 --> 06:33.360
- [Charlie scoffs]
- [Curly chuckles]

06:33.440 --> 06:34.520
Then God help you.

06:34.600 --> 06:36.040
You have tobacco, Curly?

06:36.120 --> 06:37.320
And tea and whiskey.

06:37.400 --> 06:39.640
You'll be running around like a colt.

06:39.720 --> 06:41.760
Get him aboard. I'll fill her up.

06:41.840 --> 06:42.920
[coughs]

06:43.000 --> 06:45.960
If I sleep all the way, it's Camden Town.

06:46.040 --> 06:48.080
What business do you have there?

06:48.160 --> 06:50.640
Tell Polly she's in charge
while I'm away.

06:51.480 --> 06:53.240
If I don't come back,

06:53.320 --> 06:55.320
tell her she's in charge for good.

06:59.360 --> 07:01.440
[boat engine rumbling]

07:04.240 --> 07:07.280
[slow song playing]

08:17.280 --> 08:18.840
[birds chirping]

08:24.280 --> 08:26.040
You hungry yet, Tommy?

08:32.080 --> 08:33.559
You know what, Curly?

08:34.200 --> 08:35.640
I think I am.

08:37.200 --> 08:38.760
- Where are we?
- Heathrow.

08:38.840 --> 08:40.760
One more day and we'll be there.

08:40.840 --> 08:44.240
I'll put something in the pan.
Can you steer?

08:44.320 --> 08:45.800
Give it a go.

08:49.640 --> 08:51.280
You're almost there, Tommy.

08:51.360 --> 08:52.640
[laughs]

09:23.200 --> 09:24.880
[Alfie] Fucking hell, lads.

09:30.880 --> 09:32.520
Put him down, Ollie.

09:32.600 --> 09:35.000
Put him down, mate.
He is only little.

09:38.760 --> 09:40.160
You on your own?

09:41.280 --> 09:44.160
- Seems so.
- Well, you're a brave lad, ain't you?

09:44.240 --> 09:46.400
You want to take a look
at my bakery?

09:47.480 --> 09:50.280
We bake all sorts here, mate, yeah.

09:50.360 --> 09:53.280
Did you know we bake
over 10,000 loaves a week?

09:53.360 --> 09:55.040
Can you believe it?

09:55.120 --> 09:59.320
We bake the white bread,
we bake the brown bread.

09:59.400 --> 10:02.800
We bake all sorts.
Would you like to try some?

10:06.280 --> 10:07.440
Bread?

10:08.520 --> 10:09.600
Yeah?

10:10.400 --> 10:11.360
All right.

10:11.560 --> 10:13.600
What would you like,
brown or white?

10:15.360 --> 10:17.760
- Try the brown.
- Brown, right.

10:30.520 --> 10:33.160
- Not bad.
- Not bad, eh?

10:33.960 --> 10:35.080
Not bad?

10:37.600 --> 10:39.720
It's fucking awful, that stuff.

10:39.800 --> 10:43.560
The fucking brown stuff is awful.
It is for the workers. Yeah.

10:43.640 --> 10:46.520
The white stuff,
now that is for the bosses.

10:46.600 --> 10:47.840
Come, look.

10:51.320 --> 10:55.960
Well, I've heard very bad,
bad, bad things

10:56.040 --> 10:58.360
about you Birmingham people.

10:58.440 --> 10:59.880
Tsk, tsk. Eh?

11:01.200 --> 11:02.640
You're Gypsies, right?

11:04.240 --> 11:07.560
So what, do you live
in a fucking tent or a caravan?

11:07.640 --> 11:11.400
I came here to discuss business
with you, Mr. Solomons.

11:11.480 --> 11:12.800
Well,

11:13.320 --> 11:15.480
rum is for fun and fucking,
isn't it?

11:15.560 --> 11:17.040
So, whiskey,

11:17.120 --> 11:20.040
now that is for business.

11:20.120 --> 11:21.640
Let's talk first, eh?

11:26.800 --> 11:28.080
Suit yourself.

11:29.400 --> 11:32.240
They say you had your life saved
by a policeman.

11:33.360 --> 11:35.280
I have policemen on my payroll.

11:35.360 --> 11:40.120
I don't like policemen because policemen,
they can't be trusted.

11:40.200 --> 11:43.160
Mr. Sabini uses policemen
all the time.

11:43.240 --> 11:47.160
That's why he's winning the war in London
and you are losing it.

11:47.240 --> 11:49.520
A war ain't over till it's over, mate.

11:52.120 --> 11:53.640
You were in the war?

11:54.240 --> 11:55.600
[opens drawer]

11:58.240 --> 12:03.520
I once carried out my own personal form
of stigmata on an Italian.

12:05.640 --> 12:08.240
I pushed his face up against the trench

12:08.320 --> 12:11.000
and shoved a six-inch nail
up his fucking nose

12:11.080 --> 12:13.400
and I hammered it home
with a duckboard.

12:13.480 --> 12:15.280
It was fucking biblical, mate.

12:17.400 --> 12:20.800
So don't come in here
and sit there in my chair

12:20.880 --> 12:24.800
and tell me that I am losing
my war to a fucking wop.

12:25.760 --> 12:27.560
That war was a long time ago.

12:28.640 --> 12:31.160
You need to be more realistic.

12:31.240 --> 12:32.400
[closes drawer]

12:32.480 --> 12:33.720
"Realistic", yeah?

12:35.920 --> 12:36.960
"Realistic"?

12:38.080 --> 12:39.880
If you weren't losing the war,

12:39.960 --> 12:42.280
you wouldn't have sent me the telegram.

12:42.360 --> 12:46.680
Really? You forget your fucking telegram.
The telegram just said, "Hello."

12:46.880 --> 12:49.960
Face it, you want to sell me something.

12:52.160 --> 12:53.360
What?

12:53.440 --> 12:55.720
- We join forces.
- Fuck off.

12:56.800 --> 12:58.880
No! Categorical.

13:00.040 --> 13:01.520
Fucking ridiculous.

13:02.480 --> 13:03.880
Mr. Solomons.

13:05.800 --> 13:09.760
Your distillery provides
one-tenth of your income.

13:09.840 --> 13:13.960
Protection is another 10% and the rest
you make from the race tracks.

13:14.040 --> 13:15.520
[opens drawer]

13:15.600 --> 13:19.080
I know you keep a gun in the drawer,
beside the whiskey.

13:19.160 --> 13:21.760
I know you offer a deal or death.

13:26.320 --> 13:28.840
I know what I'm saying
makes you angry.

13:28.920 --> 13:30.840
But I am offering
you a solution.

13:31.560 --> 13:33.360
You see, Mr. Sabini

13:33.440 --> 13:36.160
is running all your bookies
off your courses.

13:36.240 --> 13:37.520
[Alfie clears throat]

13:37.600 --> 13:41.960
And he is closing down
the premises that take your rum.

13:42.040 --> 13:44.760
And people don't trust
your protection anymore.

13:46.520 --> 13:48.640
You shot Billy Kimber, right?

13:50.960 --> 13:53.400
You did, you fucking shot him.
That's you.

13:54.400 --> 13:56.400
You fucking betrayed him, mate.

13:56.480 --> 13:57.960
So it'll be appropriate

13:58.040 --> 14:01.120
to do what I'm thinking
in my head to you right now.

14:03.120 --> 14:06.160
I can offer you 100 good men.

14:06.240 --> 14:08.400
All with weapons.

14:08.480 --> 14:10.800
And a new relationship
with the police.

14:10.880 --> 14:12.240
Intelligence.

14:13.160 --> 14:16.680
Intelligence is a very valuable thing,
ain't it, my friend?

14:16.760 --> 14:19.400
And usually it comes far too fucking late.

14:20.000 --> 14:22.520
[cocks gun]

14:22.600 --> 14:25.920
Let's say I shot you already,
right, in the fucking face.

14:27.480 --> 14:31.320
And the bullet goes bone, mush,
bone, cabinet over there.

14:31.400 --> 14:33.040
Which is a shame, isn't it?

14:33.120 --> 14:35.920
That cabinet is fucked.
I gotta get shot of it.

14:36.000 --> 14:38.240
So, what I do is this.

14:39.880 --> 14:41.520
It's fucking simple, mate.

14:48.760 --> 14:51.480
I cut that cabinet in half,
don't I?

14:51.560 --> 14:55.080
I do, I'll just cut the cabinet,
I cut.

14:58.000 --> 15:01.280
I cut the cabinet literally in half, mate.

15:01.360 --> 15:03.760
And I take one half of the cabinet,

15:03.840 --> 15:07.440
I put it into a barrel and
take the other half of the cabinet

15:07.520 --> 15:10.200
and all its pieces put that
in another barrel.

15:10.280 --> 15:13.640
And I send this barrel off
to Mandalay

15:13.720 --> 15:16.600
and the other barrel off to
somewhere like...

15:16.720 --> 15:18.120
I don't know.

15:19.520 --> 15:21.160
Timbuktu.

15:21.240 --> 15:23.400
- Have you ever been?
- No.

15:23.480 --> 15:24.640
No?

15:25.920 --> 15:27.200
Would you like to go?

15:29.120 --> 15:30.360
No.

15:32.200 --> 15:36.360
I always thought you'd have a great, big
fucking gold ring in your nose.

15:39.920 --> 15:41.480
I am sorry. Go on.

15:42.440 --> 15:43.880
Tell us your plan.

15:46.320 --> 15:47.640
[car horn honking]

16:01.120 --> 16:04.080
- Ada.
- How the fuck did you find me?

16:04.160 --> 16:06.040
Are you reading Polly's letters?

16:06.120 --> 16:07.760
Polly showed me the letter.

16:07.840 --> 16:10.080
You think you're safe because you moved flat.
But you're not.

16:10.200 --> 16:11.520
Polly thinks the same.

16:11.600 --> 16:12.800
Will you understand?

16:12.880 --> 16:14.720
I want you all out of my life.

16:14.800 --> 16:17.400
Ada, look at us, eh?

16:18.520 --> 16:19.920
And it'll happen again.

16:20.000 --> 16:21.800
Next time I'll be ready.

16:21.880 --> 16:24.600
Let them try again.
I'll shoot their balls off.

16:24.680 --> 16:25.960
Ada, put the gun away.

16:26.040 --> 16:28.720
- Get away. I'm late for work.
- All right.

16:28.800 --> 16:29.960
Fine.

16:30.840 --> 16:33.760
Just take this. Take it.

16:33.840 --> 16:37.600
Where you're living now is
on the edge of Sabini's territory.

16:37.680 --> 16:39.760
All the coppers there work for him.

16:39.840 --> 16:42.680
I've got a lot of money coming in.
No banks.

16:42.760 --> 16:45.320
My accountant says
to buy property for cash

16:45.400 --> 16:47.840
and put it in the name
of a family member.

16:49.560 --> 16:52.280
A house? A whole house?

16:52.360 --> 16:53.640
It's all yours.

16:54.640 --> 16:57.480
Four stories, eight bedrooms,

16:57.560 --> 17:02.760
kitchen, scullery, and rooms for a maid
if your political conscience will allow.

17:07.359 --> 17:09.040
At least go and have a look?

17:28.400 --> 17:30.480
- [scoffs]
- [door opens]

17:32.320 --> 17:34.160
Shoot the fucking lot of them.

17:34.240 --> 17:36.520
[footsteps approaching]

17:36.600 --> 17:40.160
[clears throat] Mr. Sabini?
The police are here.

17:42.480 --> 17:44.240
Tell them to wipe their feet.

17:56.440 --> 18:00.040
So why do I
have to tell you everything?

18:00.120 --> 18:03.160
We can't search every train
that comes into London.

18:03.240 --> 18:05.760
They don't use trains.
They use boats.

18:07.080 --> 18:10.200
The boatmen are all Gypsies.
He's a fucking Gypsy.

18:10.280 --> 18:12.560
Why do we've to tell them everything?

18:12.680 --> 18:14.680
Okay, we can't search every boat.

18:18.680 --> 18:22.640
Lucky for me, I have a boy
in the Jew's rum house.

18:23.600 --> 18:25.720
The Gypsy went to meet Solomons.

18:25.800 --> 18:29.720
And after, they drank whiskey
and shook hands.

18:29.800 --> 18:32.080
And then Alfie gave him
some salt

18:32.160 --> 18:35.240
or some such kike thing
that means peace.

18:36.600 --> 18:39.320
What exactly do you want us
to do, Mr. Sabini?

18:42.160 --> 18:43.880
[sniffing]

18:47.880 --> 18:50.520
Did you bring dog shit in here
on your shoes?

18:53.800 --> 18:55.120
Check. Have a look.

18:56.600 --> 18:57.600
[sniffs]

18:57.680 --> 18:59.000
I can smell something.

18:59.080 --> 19:01.520
You've got photographs of Tommy Shelby?

19:01.600 --> 19:03.240
We have military ID photos

19:03.320 --> 19:05.920
from his time in the
Warwickshire Yeomanry.

19:06.000 --> 19:07.280
Shelby won medals.

19:08.560 --> 19:10.360
[speaking Italian]

19:10.440 --> 19:14.120
Listen to these fucking soldiers
sticking together... Jesus...

19:14.200 --> 19:17.480
Why do we have to listen
to this English shit...

19:19.360 --> 19:22.760
Okay. So you've got
his fucking photo.

19:23.720 --> 19:26.240
If he shows his face
in Camden Town

19:26.320 --> 19:28.680
your coppers lift him.
All right?

19:30.160 --> 19:31.400
Yeah.

19:32.040 --> 19:36.440
Sometimes when I smell something,
it's something that's not real.

19:36.520 --> 19:39.840
It's something more like
a premonition.

19:39.920 --> 19:42.760
It's like sometimes
I smell something

19:42.840 --> 19:44.840
but it's not a smell,
it's just...

19:45.800 --> 19:47.400
Something isn't right.

19:47.480 --> 19:50.720
Mr. Sabini, we'll do everything to deal
with this man.

19:52.040 --> 19:53.040
Deal?

19:55.560 --> 19:57.040
Did you say, "Do a deal"?

19:58.080 --> 19:59.440
I didn't mean that.

20:00.880 --> 20:02.120
[Sabini] Fuck.

20:03.200 --> 20:04.440
Fuck.

20:05.960 --> 20:07.640
The world's on a tilt.

20:07.720 --> 20:09.520
You've been fucking got at.

20:09.600 --> 20:11.040
You're being ridiculous.

20:11.120 --> 20:13.120
Somebody's fucking sat you down.

20:13.200 --> 20:15.760
[angrily] Somebody's fucking
talked to you.

20:15.840 --> 20:17.760
- Mr. Sabini...
- We should go...

20:17.840 --> 20:21.320
This fucking Gypsy has sat down
with the fucking coppers...

20:21.400 --> 20:23.760
- Mr. Sabini.
- [Sabini shouting angrily]

20:23.840 --> 20:28.080
You're taking it with both fucking hands!
Off of me and off of him!

20:29.840 --> 20:33.440
Okay. So now you fucking follow Shelby.

20:33.520 --> 20:36.440
You fucking follow him
and you finish him.

20:38.280 --> 20:39.680
[shouts]

20:41.040 --> 20:44.400
- [mumbling unintelligibly]
- [bell tolling]

20:54.000 --> 20:55.880
Oh, shit!

21:24.520 --> 21:26.800
[people chattering]

21:32.360 --> 21:34.280
What the bloody hell
is going on?

21:36.920 --> 21:39.080
When did you get back?

21:39.160 --> 21:42.160
I didn't want to miss your birthday, Pol.

21:42.240 --> 21:43.320
Back to work.

21:43.400 --> 21:44.600
How do you know?

21:44.680 --> 21:46.000
Nobody ever knows.

21:46.080 --> 21:48.520
It's different this year.

21:48.600 --> 21:50.680
John, Finn, bring the car around.

21:50.760 --> 21:51.760
Where we going?

21:51.840 --> 21:53.840
To unwrap your birthday present.

21:53.920 --> 21:55.080
After you.

22:04.720 --> 22:05.880
Good morning!

22:19.080 --> 22:21.720
You said you were gonna
buy Ada a house.

22:21.800 --> 22:23.640
Yep, that's right, I did.

22:25.200 --> 22:27.400
Just had a bit of cash
left over.

22:29.120 --> 22:30.560
This is ours?

22:32.040 --> 22:34.440
No. Polly,

22:34.520 --> 22:35.640
this is yours.

22:37.760 --> 22:39.320
Because you deserve it.

22:48.000 --> 22:50.120
What do I do with all these rooms?

22:53.400 --> 22:55.440
Well, you could, uh,

22:55.520 --> 22:57.520
relax for one.

22:58.600 --> 23:00.320
Come here at weekends.

23:02.200 --> 23:04.360
It has a garden. Eh?

23:06.240 --> 23:07.920
You love gardens.

23:08.000 --> 23:09.720
You can grow roses, Pol.

23:09.800 --> 23:12.240
I don't know. Have a piano.

23:12.320 --> 23:14.920
Have people round.
They can have a singsong.

23:15.040 --> 23:18.360
- God help the bloody neighbours.
- Fuck the neighbours.

23:22.600 --> 23:24.080
Welcome home, Pol.

23:36.240 --> 23:38.800
Arthur, why don't you
take the boys outside,

23:38.880 --> 23:40.200
wait by the car?

23:50.000 --> 23:51.520
[clears throat]

23:51.600 --> 23:52.880
Pol,

23:54.160 --> 23:56.720
I know you haven't been happy
for a while.

24:00.080 --> 24:01.520
And I know why.

24:07.480 --> 24:09.200
Esme is all right, you know?

24:11.800 --> 24:14.080
She's got a good heart, she has.

24:16.720 --> 24:20.440
I have spoken to her
and she told me.

24:25.520 --> 24:27.480
Told you what?

24:27.560 --> 24:30.480
She told me what it is
that would make you happy.

24:34.000 --> 24:36.440
I've spoken to our contacts
in the police.

24:36.520 --> 24:38.840
They have contacts
in the Council.

24:38.920 --> 24:42.400
They have contacts with people
who keep the parish records.

24:43.920 --> 24:45.440
Records of adoptions.

24:47.000 --> 24:49.160
And of confidential forced removals.

24:50.280 --> 24:51.800
Now, with your permission,

24:53.160 --> 24:55.400
I'd like to grease a few palms

24:55.480 --> 24:58.600
and take a look at the records
they never showed you.

25:02.920 --> 25:04.800
Pol,

25:04.880 --> 25:07.320
I am going to find your son
and daughter

25:07.400 --> 25:09.560
and I'm gonna bring them home.

25:09.640 --> 25:11.600
That's what this house is for.

25:11.680 --> 25:15.440
So that you can bring your family home
where they belong.

25:26.920 --> 25:28.560
We're moving up, Pol.

25:50.640 --> 25:52.840
[Karl laughing]

25:55.000 --> 25:57.000
Go, have a look what you think.

26:00.640 --> 26:03.440
[Karl laughing
and running around]

26:03.520 --> 26:06.560
- Can you hear that?
- [Karl replies unintelligibly]

26:06.640 --> 26:08.440
Silence. At last.

26:10.800 --> 26:12.080
And it belongs to us.

26:12.160 --> 26:14.000
[Karl replies unintelligibly]

26:14.760 --> 26:16.960
Mummy... [speaks unintelligibly]

26:17.720 --> 26:19.080
What is in there?

26:50.440 --> 26:52.240
You're still here?

26:52.320 --> 26:55.640
Well, there's a lot coming in
and a lot going out.

26:55.720 --> 26:59.120
As long as there is more
coming in, we're all right.

27:00.320 --> 27:02.560
This came for you today.

27:02.640 --> 27:04.960
It's all the way from America.
New York.

27:05.040 --> 27:07.320
Somewhere called Poughkeepsie.

27:07.400 --> 27:10.080
The decorators doing the Garrison
want paying.

27:10.160 --> 27:12.240
Arthur pulled a gun
when they asked.

27:12.320 --> 27:14.480
They've stopped. Everything's green.

27:14.560 --> 27:15.960
All right, just pay it.

27:16.040 --> 27:18.720
And we need to put an advert
in the Mail.

27:18.800 --> 27:21.480
"Garrison, Grand Reopening."

27:21.560 --> 27:25.360
You said there's a letter that had
to go today. Special delivery.

27:25.440 --> 27:27.440
I wrote it in the diary.

27:27.520 --> 27:29.240
Yes...

27:29.320 --> 27:31.440
I haven't written it out yet.

27:31.520 --> 27:34.080
You can just tell me
and I'll write it down.

27:34.160 --> 27:35.560
I've learned shorthand.

27:36.280 --> 27:37.280
Fair enough.

27:37.360 --> 27:38.480
[clears throat]

27:39.920 --> 27:41.400
Dear Mr. Churchill...

27:46.040 --> 27:47.600
Has your pencil broken?

27:49.320 --> 27:51.560
No. No. Do go on.

27:51.640 --> 27:53.920
- [clears throat]
- Dear Mr. Churchill.

27:55.280 --> 27:59.320
I have been approached
by an agent of the Crown

27:59.400 --> 28:03.400
to carry out a task,
as yet unspecified.

28:04.520 --> 28:08.560
His name is Major Campbell
and I believe he reports to you.

28:10.200 --> 28:14.000
Therefore, I decided
to make direct contact with you

28:14.080 --> 28:17.480
to make sure that certain things
are clearly understood...

28:17.560 --> 28:20.400
[Churchill] "Mr. Churchill,
you should know that

28:20.480 --> 28:24.000
I am a former British soldier.
If you look at my war record,

28:24.080 --> 28:27.920
you will see that I fought bravely
at Verdun and the Somme.

28:28.000 --> 28:30.760
Also you will see
that my actions at Mon

28:30.840 --> 28:33.440
saved thousands of Allied lives.

28:33.520 --> 28:36.680
I know that you resigned
your ministerial position

28:36.760 --> 28:41.240
and the safety of an office to go and
fight on the front line with the men.

28:41.320 --> 28:44.280
I read that you fought bravely,
Mr. Churchill.

28:44.360 --> 28:47.880
Therefore, I hope I will be
treated in any dealings we have

28:47.960 --> 28:49.720
with a degree of respect,

28:49.800 --> 28:51.920
soldier to soldier.

28:52.000 --> 28:56.240
My demands are slight and my sacrifice
in service of my country

28:56.320 --> 28:58.920
will no doubt once again be great.

28:59.000 --> 29:01.640
Yours sincerely, Thomas Shelby."

29:03.080 --> 29:06.560
Military Medal and the DCM.

29:08.440 --> 29:10.680
Distinguished Conduct Medal.

29:12.160 --> 29:15.320
Hands up in this room
those who were at the Somme.

29:17.440 --> 29:20.080
Those who were at
the Battle of Verdun.

29:26.080 --> 29:28.760
Thomas Shelby was a tunneler.

29:31.400 --> 29:33.560
His demand is rather amusing.

29:33.640 --> 29:35.840
He has asked that the colonial office

29:35.920 --> 29:38.440
grant him an Empire export license.

29:38.520 --> 29:39.920
[men laugh]

29:40.000 --> 29:41.640
Specifically a license

29:41.720 --> 29:45.920
covering India, Malay Peninsula,
Canada and Russia.

29:46.000 --> 29:49.560
He plans to transport
certain manufactured goods

29:49.640 --> 29:51.880
from Birmingham to the Poplar Docks.

29:51.960 --> 29:54.120
And you plan to agree
to this demand?

29:54.200 --> 29:57.640
Dear God.
Major Campbell.

29:59.200 --> 30:04.720
We will be asking this man to carry out
an assassination on behalf of the Crown.

30:04.800 --> 30:07.680
Once more risking his life.

30:07.760 --> 30:11.920
These demands by comparison
are slight and easily met

30:12.000 --> 30:15.880
with a few quiet words over lunch
with the appropriate ministers.

30:15.960 --> 30:19.640
Sir, with the greatest respect,

30:19.720 --> 30:22.920
Thomas Shelby is a murdering,

30:23.000 --> 30:26.080
cutthroat mongrel gangster.

30:27.400 --> 30:28.880
And yet

30:28.960 --> 30:32.000
the tunnels were dug
beneath our feet

30:32.080 --> 30:35.440
to silence the guns
pointed at our heads.

30:38.280 --> 30:41.160
Get Thomas Shelby's details
from Campbell

30:41.240 --> 30:44.440
and get some kind of
royal appointment stamp.

30:44.520 --> 30:47.320
- Temporary.
- Temporary.

30:47.400 --> 30:49.880
Just until the deed is done.

30:51.000 --> 30:53.280
Agreed, Major Campbell?

30:53.360 --> 30:56.320
And then I can proceed
as discussed.

30:59.320 --> 31:00.840
Agreed, sir.

31:15.240 --> 31:17.440
[boy] Come on, let's go inside.

31:19.600 --> 31:21.120
Here, pass the ball.

31:24.520 --> 31:25.680
Come on.

31:26.760 --> 31:29.720
[woman] Come on in, boys.
Did you have a good game?

31:29.800 --> 31:32.320
[boy] I am starving. Anything for lunch?

31:32.400 --> 31:35.560
[woman] In you go.
Go in and wash your hands, boys.

31:35.640 --> 31:37.560
[boy] Come on, mister.

31:37.640 --> 31:38.880
Mrs. Johnson?

31:39.640 --> 31:42.840
Yes. Who are you?

31:42.920 --> 31:46.480
I am from Birmingham Council.
Bordesley Parish.

31:46.560 --> 31:48.360
No one wrote to me.

31:48.440 --> 31:49.840
What do you want?

31:49.920 --> 31:53.040
I would like to talk
about your son, about Henry.

31:53.120 --> 31:54.400
Can I come in?

31:54.480 --> 31:57.920
I'd rather you didn't.
He doesn't like to talk about this.

31:58.440 --> 31:59.640
I see.

32:01.200 --> 32:04.880
So what does Henry know about
his real identity, Mrs. Johnson?

32:05.120 --> 32:07.640
I only deal with Mr. Ross
from the agency.

32:07.720 --> 32:11.080
And he only ever writes.
So why are you here in person?

32:11.160 --> 32:13.920
The boy is approaching
his eighteenth birthday.

32:14.760 --> 32:16.120
This isn't right.

32:17.240 --> 32:20.280
You are not from the Council.
Something isn't right.

32:20.360 --> 32:22.440
What does he know, Mrs. Johnson?

32:24.840 --> 32:27.320
He knows his mother couldn't cope.

32:27.400 --> 32:30.640
She drank too much,
she used opium,

32:30.720 --> 32:32.680
she used to beat him.

32:32.760 --> 32:34.280
But that isn't the truth.

32:34.360 --> 32:37.320
Look, you should come back
when my husband is here.

32:37.400 --> 32:39.160
Does he know his real name?

32:39.240 --> 32:41.520
His real name is Johnson.

32:41.600 --> 32:44.360
Henry Johnson.
Now, I would like you to go away

32:44.440 --> 32:46.680
and come back when my husband is here.

32:46.760 --> 32:50.280
Truth is he was taken from his mother
without her permission.

32:50.360 --> 32:52.600
Henry, go back inside, please.

32:52.680 --> 32:54.880
- Who are you?
- Please, Henry, go on!

32:54.960 --> 32:56.760
Your real name's Michael Gray.

32:56.840 --> 32:59.280
- No!
- Your real mother wants to see you.

32:59.360 --> 33:01.040
Her address is on this card.

33:01.120 --> 33:03.240
- She just wants to talk.
- Go away!

33:03.320 --> 33:04.680
She just wants to talk.

33:04.760 --> 33:06.680
Go away and leave us alone!

33:12.040 --> 33:14.040
Come on, let's go back inside.

33:14.120 --> 33:15.920
I'll get you something to eat.

33:16.760 --> 33:18.120
Come on, Henry.

33:20.120 --> 33:21.960
Come on. Come on.

33:23.560 --> 33:25.680
[King Maine]
Call yourself a Shelby?

33:25.760 --> 33:28.040
Fucking hit him, Finn.

33:28.120 --> 33:30.080
Hit him, that's right.

33:30.200 --> 33:31.920
All right, Isiah, your turn.

33:32.800 --> 33:36.000
Finn, call yourself a Shelby?
Hit him for fuck's sake.

33:38.360 --> 33:39.560
Hit him.

33:41.000 --> 33:42.680
[man shouting angrily]

33:44.520 --> 33:47.320
[King Maine] Get him out of there,
get him back!

33:47.400 --> 33:50.920
Get him back for fuck's sake!
Get him out of there!

33:51.800 --> 33:53.320
[Arthur panting]

34:05.280 --> 34:06.360
[shouts]

34:08.159 --> 34:10.520
[indistinct chatter]

34:16.280 --> 34:19.800
[speaking inaudibly]

34:25.920 --> 34:28.679
[footsteps approaching]

34:46.880 --> 34:48.400
All right. What happened?

34:48.920 --> 34:50.960
It was a fair fight.

34:51.040 --> 34:54.000
Yeah. Arthur just caught him
with a good one.

34:58.000 --> 34:59.560
I saw the body.

34:59.640 --> 35:03.040
Don't fucking lie to me.
I saw the body. What happened?

35:04.800 --> 35:07.520
I'm not family,
I'm saying nothing.

35:12.480 --> 35:13.600
Arthur,

35:14.920 --> 35:16.720
he's blown a few times lately.

35:17.840 --> 35:19.640
Six, seven.

35:21.600 --> 35:23.720
It's like he's not there
in the head.

35:24.440 --> 35:26.120
He can't even hear "Stop."

35:26.200 --> 35:27.840
Or even his own name.

35:30.000 --> 35:31.640
And then he cries.

35:37.400 --> 35:38.760
Right, listen to me.

35:39.920 --> 35:41.760
That's two fucking stories.

35:43.240 --> 35:45.600
Your brother killed a boy.

35:46.560 --> 35:49.560
There were witnesses,
there will be questions,

35:49.640 --> 35:53.600
get your stories straight.
It was an accident. Fuck off.

36:00.960 --> 36:05.320
[Thomas] I spoke to the doctor.
He said the kid probably had a weak heart.

36:06.240 --> 36:07.960
We'll look after his mother.

36:09.480 --> 36:10.640
It will be fine.

36:11.840 --> 36:14.600
Polly said I should've stuck
with the medicine.

36:16.880 --> 36:18.680
I said that you know best.

36:21.920 --> 36:24.000
Head's like a fucking boat, Tommy.

36:27.040 --> 36:29.640
Full of heavy cargo.
Like coal or iron.

36:30.720 --> 36:32.680
Sometimes it slips to one end.

36:35.080 --> 36:36.920
And the boat tips.

36:39.160 --> 36:40.960
I can feel it slipping.

36:42.560 --> 36:44.640
And I can feel the boat tipping.

36:47.680 --> 36:50.120
But there ain't nothing
I can do about it.

36:52.880 --> 36:59.160
It's like my fucking head's just like this
big fucking barge.

36:59.240 --> 37:03.080
And it just fucking drifts

37:03.160 --> 37:06.440
in and out, in and out...

37:06.520 --> 37:08.880
We've been home
a long time now, Arthur.

37:09.960 --> 37:12.040
Been home a long time.

37:14.040 --> 37:16.760
- I thought you were all right.
- Yeah, well...

37:17.760 --> 37:21.760
[stuttering] I don't think I am, Tommy.

37:25.920 --> 37:27.680
Take this fucking thing away.

37:34.520 --> 37:36.360
Just fuck off, Arthur!

37:37.880 --> 37:39.360
You know what?

37:42.920 --> 37:46.160
I have had enough.
I have fucking had enough.

37:46.840 --> 37:48.840
Just fuck off!

37:52.720 --> 37:55.720
Supposed to treat you
like a fucking kid again, eh?

37:56.600 --> 38:00.080
Keep you away from guns
and fucking ropes. Is that it?

38:00.840 --> 38:03.600
- You think I haven't got enough on?
- Stop it.

38:03.680 --> 38:06.360
Arthur, you think
I haven't got enough on?

38:06.440 --> 38:08.280
- Stop it.
- The war is done.

38:08.360 --> 38:09.600
Shut the door on it!

38:09.680 --> 38:12.240
- Stop it.
- Shut the door on it like I did!

38:12.600 --> 38:14.880
- Like you did?
- Can you do that? Yeah?

38:14.960 --> 38:17.000
Like I did.
Shut the fucking door.

38:17.080 --> 38:20.040
But I'm not fucking you, am I?

38:20.120 --> 38:21.840
I am not fucking you!

38:21.920 --> 38:24.480
Everyone fucking knows it!

38:26.280 --> 38:27.720
God!

38:30.680 --> 38:32.280
Look at yourself.

38:33.800 --> 38:35.080
Look at yourself.

38:38.360 --> 38:42.840
Take a long, hard fucking look
at yourself.

38:44.600 --> 38:45.760
[sobbing]

38:52.480 --> 38:54.560
[door opens and closes]

38:55.360 --> 38:57.000
[continues sobbing]

38:57.080 --> 38:58.320
[door opens again]

39:07.920 --> 39:09.720
[door opens and slams shut]

39:14.040 --> 39:16.320
[inaudible]

39:29.400 --> 39:31.320
[bell ringing]

39:31.400 --> 39:32.640
Time for lunch, sir.

39:32.720 --> 39:36.080
I thought we could eat at the desk
like the old days.

39:42.680 --> 39:44.520
You remembered.

39:44.600 --> 39:46.360
Tongue and pickle, sir.

39:46.440 --> 39:49.880
But a single stroke of mustard,
that's the thing.

39:49.960 --> 39:52.160
- Hmm.
- The fine detail.

39:55.320 --> 39:56.640
Speaking of detail,

39:58.120 --> 40:03.800
how much is Thomas Shelby paying you
in bribes per week, hm?

40:07.760 --> 40:09.680
Actually, I already know.

40:09.760 --> 40:12.080
Two pounds a week.
Eight pounds a month.

40:12.160 --> 40:14.800
Plus extras for specific favors.

40:15.960 --> 40:19.000
When I left,
you were an honest man.

40:19.080 --> 40:22.360
And now you're on
the Peaky Blinders' payroll.

40:25.280 --> 40:27.480
Shelby is a worm

40:27.560 --> 40:30.560
that feeds off
the rotten parts of your mind.

40:30.640 --> 40:32.960
He gets in through your ear.

40:33.040 --> 40:34.680
With a whisper.

40:34.760 --> 40:39.840
He crawls in over your tongue
as you lie to the judge and the pastor.

40:41.640 --> 40:42.960
Mr. Moss,

40:44.240 --> 40:48.160
we are entering
a bloody phase of a bloody war,

40:48.240 --> 40:50.600
no quarter asked nor given.

40:52.160 --> 40:54.280
But as of this moment,

40:54.360 --> 40:57.800
you are now back
on the side of the angels.

40:59.440 --> 41:04.440
Shelby thinks you work for him,
but in truth, you work for me.

41:05.480 --> 41:08.320
And I will use you mercilessly.

41:10.440 --> 41:12.840
[rhythmic clanging outside]

41:13.640 --> 41:15.440
[door opens]

41:58.600 --> 42:01.680
Polly, these are the files
for Michael and Anna Gray.

42:03.040 --> 42:05.000
As you can see, one is white,

42:06.520 --> 42:08.000
and one is black.

42:12.920 --> 42:14.600
What you saw in your dreams

42:15.640 --> 42:16.880
was right.

42:19.680 --> 42:21.360
Your daughter is dead.

42:33.880 --> 42:36.400
Uh... You do it.
I can't read it.

42:41.560 --> 42:44.680
After Anna was taken from you,
she was put with

42:45.920 --> 42:49.400
the family of a railway stationmaster
in Stafford.

42:51.800 --> 42:54.120
She never settled.

42:55.280 --> 42:57.520
So she kept running away.

43:00.200 --> 43:02.480
She got on a train
to Birmingham once.

43:03.760 --> 43:06.920
Eventually, they sent her
to Australia.

43:07.000 --> 43:09.800
Where she died of something
called Spring fever.

43:26.600 --> 43:28.520
So my little Anna

43:29.800 --> 43:32.280
traveled all the way back
across the world

43:32.360 --> 43:34.080
to be with me in my dreams.

43:37.440 --> 43:39.200
She came all by herself.

43:41.680 --> 43:43.160
Well,

43:43.240 --> 43:45.720
that's one train
they couldn't get her off.

43:50.320 --> 43:51.680
[sniffles]

43:53.920 --> 43:55.120
But, Pol,

43:56.040 --> 43:57.560
Michael is alive.

44:05.040 --> 44:06.320
He's in England.

44:10.200 --> 44:12.520
And I know where he lives.
I went there.

44:14.240 --> 44:15.880
- But, Pol...
- And?

44:18.080 --> 44:19.840
Okay, Polly, listen to me...

44:21.520 --> 44:23.320
Just listen to me, please.

44:24.760 --> 44:26.480
Pol!

44:26.560 --> 44:29.400
The woman that he calls mother

44:29.480 --> 44:31.200
will never let him come here.

44:33.560 --> 44:35.600
Just tell me where he is, Tommy.

44:37.240 --> 44:39.520
Polly, if you go there,

44:39.600 --> 44:41.000
and you get mad,

44:41.080 --> 44:43.720
and she gets mad
and she calls the police,

44:43.800 --> 44:48.000
they will never let you anywhere near him
and the boy will get scared.

44:48.760 --> 44:50.600
That is what will happen, Pol.

44:52.800 --> 44:54.000
Polly.

44:55.360 --> 44:56.240
Polly...

44:56.320 --> 44:57.800
Just tell me where he is.

45:02.600 --> 45:05.160
- Tell me where he is, Thomas.
- Polly.

45:09.200 --> 45:11.200
Pulling a gun...

45:11.280 --> 45:13.560
Pulling a gun
is why I can't tell you.

45:17.480 --> 45:19.800
Pol, I am sorry.

45:19.880 --> 45:22.120
You're gonna have to
wait till he's 18.

45:22.200 --> 45:24.920
Till he's old enough
to make his own decision.

45:30.680 --> 45:31.800
[angrily] "Wait"?

45:32.800 --> 45:34.520
You tell me where he is!

45:40.480 --> 45:42.520
If you shoot me,
you'll never know.

45:47.560 --> 45:48.840
[gunshot]

45:53.160 --> 45:54.480
[door opens]

45:54.560 --> 45:55.600
[door slams shut]

45:55.840 --> 45:57.840
[upbeat music playing]

45:58.720 --> 46:01.040
[people speaking chattering]

46:23.200 --> 46:25.920
[Finn] Mickey! Hurry up!
The party's starting!

46:31.120 --> 46:33.480
- Carry on.
- Finn, where are you going?

46:33.560 --> 46:34.920
To talk to Arthur.

46:38.040 --> 46:40.800
[boys chattering excitedly]

47:02.080 --> 47:04.640
- Arthur?
- Go away.

47:06.040 --> 47:08.480
What is wrong?
Why aren't you ready?

47:08.560 --> 47:11.440
- For what?
- The Garrison.

47:14.000 --> 47:16.680
The reopening of your pub.
It's tonight.

47:18.040 --> 47:19.600
Tommy don't want me there.

47:19.680 --> 47:21.760
It was Tommy who told me
to get you.

47:21.840 --> 47:25.360
Says it's your name above that door.
You've got to be there.

47:25.440 --> 47:26.440
No.

47:27.120 --> 47:28.560
No, look at me.

47:29.560 --> 47:32.000
I am staying away. Go away.

47:32.960 --> 47:34.360
Get out.

47:46.960 --> 47:48.840
I told you to go away.

47:53.920 --> 47:55.400
Sometimes

47:57.520 --> 47:59.840
when we boys are feeling blue,

48:01.480 --> 48:02.920
this is what we do.

48:04.000 --> 48:05.400
It's called Tokyo.

48:29.920 --> 48:33.120
It is good stuff, Arthur,
it will help you.

48:35.760 --> 48:37.360
For special occasions.

48:42.080 --> 48:44.040
[snorting]

48:49.200 --> 48:50.800
[upbeat music playing]

49:03.880 --> 49:05.560
My fucking Garrison!

49:05.640 --> 49:07.640
[people chattering,
music playing]

49:07.720 --> 49:09.000
Take that. Take that.

49:09.080 --> 49:11.680
Here you are, Lizzie,
let me get you a light.

49:11.760 --> 49:13.200
There you go, darling.

49:13.280 --> 49:15.040
I'll be with you in a second.

49:18.160 --> 49:19.800
Look at the place.

49:21.880 --> 49:23.000
Are you all right?

49:23.080 --> 49:25.560
The busier we are,
the faster time passes!

49:25.640 --> 49:26.800
Yeah?

49:28.360 --> 49:30.000
Arthur.

49:30.080 --> 49:33.080
Tokyo is fuel for races, eh?

49:33.160 --> 49:35.640
You've seen a doped horse
after a race.

49:35.720 --> 49:37.320
Oh, yeah.

49:37.400 --> 49:40.520
Grand openings
and race days only, brother.

49:40.600 --> 49:41.800
All right!

49:43.840 --> 49:45.440
Boys.

49:45.520 --> 49:46.800
All right, men?

49:48.680 --> 49:51.120
Finn, give me that.

49:52.320 --> 49:54.280
Keep him off the whiskey, John.

51:12.600 --> 51:13.960
[door opens]

51:24.640 --> 51:25.720
Pol?

51:29.680 --> 51:31.640
[people chattering and laughing]

51:31.720 --> 51:33.720
[upbeat music playing]

51:34.800 --> 51:37.520
[laughing loudly]

51:41.400 --> 51:43.560
Ada. I didn't know
you were coming.

51:43.640 --> 51:44.920
Hello, Aunt Polly.

51:47.960 --> 51:49.880
[indistinct chatter]

51:53.480 --> 51:55.760
Oh, Ada, why don't you come home?

51:55.840 --> 51:57.080
Hello, Ada.

51:57.160 --> 51:59.080
[Polly stops talking abruptly]

52:01.880 --> 52:02.960
Tommy.

52:04.200 --> 52:05.760
What do you think, eh?

52:05.840 --> 52:07.320
It's very, um...

52:07.400 --> 52:09.160
- Gold?
- Yeah.

52:10.760 --> 52:11.840
[John] Hello, Ada.

52:11.920 --> 52:14.160
Hello, trouble, how are you doing?

52:14.240 --> 52:17.200
- Spilling my drink.
- Hey, Arthur, look who it is.

52:18.360 --> 52:20.520
So what do you want me to say
to her?

52:20.600 --> 52:24.520
Just talk to her.
Nobody can get through to her.

52:24.600 --> 52:26.280
Tell her I have no choice.

52:27.680 --> 52:28.920
I appreciate this.

52:36.440 --> 52:39.160
- Hello, brother.
- Ada, come here, you.

52:42.400 --> 52:45.480
Polly? Why don't you come
and join us?

52:52.280 --> 52:54.080
- What?
- I'm not an idiot.

52:55.040 --> 52:57.000
He asked you to come, didn't he?

53:04.960 --> 53:08.360
I don't want conversation.
I want an address.

53:08.440 --> 53:12.520
Until I have that,
I have not got anything to say.

53:12.600 --> 53:17.280
- Well, it's a party.
- Yeah. It is!

53:22.520 --> 53:24.400
And I'm going to enjoy myself.

53:24.480 --> 53:26.200
No, Pol, don't be silly...

53:27.440 --> 53:28.920
Excuse me.

53:30.400 --> 53:32.160
Would you care to dance?

53:34.160 --> 53:36.080
It would be my pleasure.

53:43.680 --> 53:47.360
My love, the pleasure hasn't even begun.

56:00.440 --> 56:01.880
[boy] Excuse me?

56:03.480 --> 56:06.360
I'm looking for someone
called Elizabeth Gray.

56:06.440 --> 56:08.720
I was told she lived here
at number 17.

56:10.720 --> 56:13.320
I came last night,
but there was nobody here.

56:14.120 --> 56:15.200
So I waited.

56:16.280 --> 56:18.400
What do you want
with Elizabeth Gray?

56:21.560 --> 56:23.440
I think she might be my mother.

56:58.120 --> 57:01.040
[theme music playing]
